  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-El Ni&#xf1;o Response in Somalia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Somalia experiences two primary types of floods: flash floods and fluvial floods. Mostly occurring in the Deyr wet season, these floods are significantly influenced by rainfall in Ethiopia&#x2019;s Shebelle and Juba rivers catchment areas (Billi P, 2022). Thesecalamitous events lead to loss of lives, destruction to infrastructure, mass migrations, and decreases in agricultural productivity, particularly impacting Somali communities in flood-prone regions (Ahmed, 2020).
The Gu season rains of 2021 caused flashfloods and riverine flooding, affecting around 166,000 people, uprooting numerous communities, and destroying farmland (OCHA, 2021). Similar catastrophic events were witnessed in the 2023 Gu season in Bardhere District, compounding Somalia&#x2019;s existing food security issues and livelihood struggles, especially those already coping with the extended drought from Deyr 2020 to Deyr 2022.
The obliteration of critical infrastructure like roads and bridges hampers the efforts of humanitarian organizations, making it challenging to deliver necessary assistance to these flood-affected populations. The devastation intensifies as basic services such as healthcare, food, market access,and clean water become inaccessible to affected communities. The aftermath of the floods increases the risk of disease outbreaks due to contaminated water sources and inadequate sanitation facilities.
Given the severe impacts floods impose on the vulnerable Somali communities, it&apos;s clear that the quick implementation of anticipatory action is crucial, particularly in susceptible areas. The proactive measures could include establishing robust early warning systems, pre-positioning of vital emergency supplies, and prompt infrastructure repairs to reduce flood damage risk. Fostering anticipatory action could play a pivotal role in mitigating the negativeimpacts of future floods, ultimately saving lives and preserving resources in Somalia.  Somalia, particularly the district of Baladwayn in the Hirshabele state, has historically been hitbyEl Ni&#xf1;o flood events that caused significant damage to life, property, and the environment. To mitigate this, proactive steps need to be taken to prepare vulnerable communities for these potential disasters.</narrative>
  </description>
